{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw docs/reviews/wi-978/018-ADJUDICATE-d3a3531.md; rg -n -A 100 -B 3 '# Fix round 4 additions' 'C:/Projects/ai-template.wt/review-tmp/2026-11-02b-leg30/spine-change-list.md'; Get-Content -Raw docs/reviews/wi-978/015-REVIEW-A-f7809e3.md; Get-Content -Raw docs/reviews/wi-978/016-REVIEW-B-f7809e3.md; git diff --no-ext-diff HEAD~3..HEAD -- project-trajectory tests; rg -n -A 12 '\"'^'\"\\\\[design\\\\.LLR-(286|362|363)\\\\]|\"'^'\"\\\\[interface\\\\.IF-224\\\\]|\"'^'\"\\\\[requirement\\\\.SR-174\\\\]|\"'^'\"\\\\[test\\\\.TC-402\\\\]' docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml docs/requirements/interfaces.toml docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml docs/test/test-cases.toml\"","aggregated_output":"# WI-978 dispute sitting at d3a3531\n\nDISPUTE: findings=F1\n\n## F1: retiring an id live at the lane's base, then authoring it again, escapes the retirement refusal\n\n**Disclosure.** I ruled dispute sitting 008, whose FIX worded the retirement refusal as applying to \"an added id the landing tree or its parent records as retired\". The lane built exactly that, and this finding is the shape that wording left out. I also blessed SR-174's widened text and approved LLR-363 and TC-402 at sitting 013. The gap is partly in what I asked for, and this ruling closes it rather than defending it.\n\n**Observed at this range.** In `id_carrier.landing_lines`:\n- **The order of the checks.** The judgement collects the added ids, `(s, n)` live in the tip and not in the lane's merge base (`_added_ids`). It returns `[]` when there are none (line 486), and only then runs `_retired_lines` over the added set. With SR-003 live at the base and live again at the tip, the judgement is handed no added id and passes. That holds even with `docs/log.d/retired/SR-003.md` in the landing tree, and the same id treated as added is refused naming its record (called directly, both shown).\n- **No other guard.** `retire.retirement_findings` checks unreadable records, census problems, spent ids without a record and records edited after landing. Nothing reports a live row whose id holds a retirement record.\n\nSo this sequence lands, on both routes, a retirement record and a live row with a different obligation under the same identity:\n1. `retire.py SR-003`, which writes the record and deletes the row;\n2. a new, different SR-003 authored in the same lane.\n\nThe reviewer reports the merge slot landing it and the squash step exiting 0, which matches the code path above.\n\n**In scope.** §6 counts \"the content agents write into the repository (the gates exist to check it)\". The steps are the kit's own: `retire.py` is the one writer of retirements, and the row is authored by the spine author. The confusion is a natural one for a model author told to replace a row: it retires the row, then writes \"the replacement\" under the same id. It needs no forged state, no hand edit of a kit-written record, and no timing window. It is less likely than sitting 008's in-range case, since the brief's reserved ranges do not point at the number. But it is the same misuse, and the retirement record that would refuse it is already on disk at the boundary that judges the landing.\n\n**Convergence-class: a fail-open against the approved SR.**\n- SR-174, as this lane widened it and sitting 013 blessed it, promises \"an identity freed by a deletion is never re-issued\", and its acceptance says \"an identity freed by deleting its record is not re-issued to new work\".\n- Before this lane, that promise covered work items only. The lane extended it to every registry identity, so the lane owes its enforcement on the routes it built.\n- A fail-open of an approved requirement's promise on a supported route cannot be SUCCESSOR. Its fix is cheap beside the defect, so it is not `not-worth-cost`.\n\n**What the fix must do.**\n- **The refusal.** At the one landing boundary both routes share, judge the identities live in the landing tree against the retirement records, independently of the net-added set. Refuse a live id that a retirement record names, naming the id and the record, at least where the landing adds the record or changes the row. The narrower form keeps a legacy pair, already on trunk before the lane, from blocking unrelated landings.\n- **The kept refusals.** The existing added-id refusals and sitting 008's in-range case stand.\n- **The regression.** Drive a base-live retire, re-author regression through the merge slot and the hand squash, failing on the current code.\n- **The spine.** Amend LLR-363's retirement sentence (today \"An added identity that a retirement record ... names is refused\") and TC-402's matching arms through their own authority. SR-174 is unchanged, since it already states the rule.\n\nRULING: F1 FIX the landing judgement applies the retirement refusal only to net-added ids and returns before consulting retirement records when none is added, so a lane that retires an id live at its base with retire.py and authors a different row under that id lands a retired identity re-issued on both routes, against SR-174's approved non-reissue clause; judge live identities against retirement records the landing adds or whose row it changes, on both callers, with a base-live regression and the LLR-363/TC-402 amendments\n\r\n276-    now takes `_landing_ready` in place of `_merge_ready`; integrate.py's IF-173 contract text says\n277-    so. LLR-363 `code_symbol` already lists `_landing_ready`.\n278-\n279:# Fix round 4 additions\n280-\n281-40. LLR-363 `detail` (Drafted), the retirement sentence. Before:\n282-    \"An added identity that a retirement record in the landing tree or its parent names is\n283-    refused, naming the identity and the record, whether or not a reservation of the landing's\n284-    work item covers it.\"\n285-    Proposed:\n286-    \"An added identity that a retirement record in the landing tree or its parent names is\n287-    refused, naming the identity and the record, whether or not a reservation of the landing's\n288-    work item covers it. Independently of the added identities, and before a landing that adds\n289-    none passes, an identity live in the landing tree that a retirement record the landing tree\n290-    holds and its parent does not names is refused, naming the identity and the record; a\n291-    retirement record and live row both already held by the parent do not refuse the landing.\"\n292-    (The hand landing judges both its staged tree over the integration branch's tip and the\n293-    lane's tip over its merge base, as for added identities.)\n294-41. LLR-363 `code_symbol` (Drafted): gains `_reissued_ids` (id_carrier.py, tagged\n295-    `Implements: SR-174, LLR-363`), beside `_read_landing`.\n296-42. TC-402 (Drafted), the matching arms.\n297-    `method`: after \"drive a reserved identity authored, retired and authored again;\" add\n298-    \"drive an identity live at the lane's base retired and authored again in the lane, and a lane\n299-    landing beside a retirement record and live row trunk already held;\".\n300-    `expected`: after \"an identity authored, retired and authored again inside its reservation\n301-    refuses at both landings naming its retirement record;\" add \"an identity live at the lane's\n302-    base, retired and authored again in the lane, refuses at both landings naming its\n303-    retirement record, and a lane beside a retired identity and live row trunk already held\n304-    lands;\".\n305-    `evidence`: gains\n306-    `tests/test_id_landing.py::test_the_slot_refuses_an_id_its_base_held_retired_and_re_authored`,\n307-    `tests/test_id_landing.py::test_the_slot_lands_a_lane_beside_a_retired_id_trunk_already_holds_live`,\n308-    `tests/test_id_landing.py::test_the_hand_squash_refuses_an_id_its_base_held_retired_and_re_authored`.\n309-    (The in-process pin `tests/test_id_carrier.py::test_the_landing_judgement_rules_each_row_of_the_table`\n310-    gains the case `reissued-with-none-added`; it belongs to whichever TC already lists that test.)\n311-43. SR-174: unchanged (the dispute sitting rules it already states the rule).\nReviewed: f7809e313a17bf145b639922b1d84192760f7a66\n\nVerified: Reviewed the scoped full-lane diff, WI-978's complete spec and its item-16 target/owner rulings, PROCESS sections 3–6, the kit-change rubric, and the cited registry rows and their affected historical users. Hunted silent identity substitution/reuse and fail-open landing admission first, then carrier overwrite/record loss. The carrier's CAS, sequential allocation, stale-base folding, preservation of records, and the shared landing seam have substantial executable coverage. A real simultaneous reservation from two worktrees produced one success and one lost-race refusal, with no overlapping allocation. WI-978's own reservations exist and its own landing judgement passes. The supplied batch is not green in this environment; the environmental failures below are not counted as code findings. HEAD remained the reviewed commit and git status remained clean.\n\nCommands:\n\n- `git rev-parse HEAD` — `f7809e313a17bf145b639922b1d84192760f7a66` (confirmed before running checks and at completion).\n- `git diff 2f4ebb2a5e00b2be05298ea2d45591fed1b90e06...f7809e313a17bf145b639922b1d84192760f7a66 -- . ':(exclude)docs/iteration' ':(exclude)docs/reviews' ':(exclude)docs/decisions' ':(exclude)docs/plans/*/critique-*' ':(exclude)docs/log.md' ':(exclude)docs/log.d' ':(exclude)PROJECT_STATE.html' ':(exclude)docs/open-items.html' ':(exclude)docs/stage'` (stat and scoped path reads) — 45 files, 4139 insertions, 211 deletions. Get-Content/rg and targeted TOML queries read the governing text and cited rows; no whole registry or activity log was read.\n- With `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES=C:/Projects/ai-template.wt/review-tmp/2026-11-02b-leg30`, `C:/Projects/ai-template/.venv