n 2.29s`. These are expected reds: retired-ID reuse on both routes, another lane's already-landed number in a squash, speculative drain readiness, a code-only lane after a trunk deletion, export-ignore hiding a registry, a registry conflict beside the watermark, stale-base folding through the integrity floor, sequential reservations, and bump preservation. The failures were inspected for their intended assertion, not merely their exit code. The non-executable backlink correction and removal of the duplicate rendering wrapper were confirmed directly against their patches and current callers.\n- Base-host comparison, in the `2f4ebb2` script snapshot: `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider --tb=short --basetemp C:/Projects/ai-template.wt/review-tmp/2026-11-02b-leg30/gate-A/base-host-check tests/test_retire.py::test_a_shallow_clone_says_the_record_is_unverifiable tests/test_pre_commit_hook.py::test_hook_runs_end_to_end_when_sh_available` — `2 failed in 5.75s`, both with the same Git Bash `CreateFileMapping ... Win32 error 5` as the reviewed tip. All 19 failing hook cases and the shallow-clone case in the supplied batch carry that host error, before the relevant shell code executes. They are not charged to this range.\n- Unchanged new sweep test in a writable scratch repository containing the reviewed scripts/tests/registries: `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider --tb=short --basetemp <gate-A/current-sweep-tmp> tests/test_id_landing.py::test_the_extracted_sweep_equals_the_working_sweep_for_every_carrier_space` — `1 passed in 2.00s`. In the original worktree it failed at private-index `git add`, with `insufficient permission for adding an object to repository database C:/Projects/ai-template/.git/objects`; this is the remaining supplied-batch failure, not a sweep mismatch.\n- Python stdin probes through the shipped interfaces, using the real-repository fixtures under gate-A: two concurrent `trace.py --reserve-ids` subprocesses — exits `1` and `0`, loser says `another reservation moved the carrier; re-run`, carrier holds only `('SR', 6, 7, 'WI-002')`, disjoint `True`. `trace.landing_id_refusal(root, 'f7809e3', '2f4ebb2', ['WI-978'])` — `None`; `git show refs/heads/id-carrier:docs/id-watermark` confirms LLR 362–363 and TC 401–402 for WI-978.\n- Retirement-reuse probes using `retire.py`, real commits, `integrate.integrate_one`, and the actual squash `reserved-ids` step — with SR-003 already live at the claim/base, retire it through the command, commit, author a different SR-003, commit: merge slot reports `slot refusal= None` and `landed-new-obligation= True`; hand squash returns `0` and `ok reserved-ids no squash landing adds an unreserved id or lags the id carrier`. The clean probes are retained under gate-A/probe-existing-retired-id. An initial probe inherited the coordinator's loop marker and was refused for missing provenance; the clean probes removed that ambient marker before claim, as the test suite's fixture does. No provenance record was edited or bypassed.\n- Byte measurements against the base: PROCESS.md `97962 -> 98419`, delta `+457`; shipped byte-budget skill `4559 -> 4527`, delta `-32`, matching their stamps. `git status --porcelain` — empty.\n\nDone-when coverage and independent semantic decisions:\n\n- Spine meaning, trace fields, and IF-101: inspected SR-174, LLR-362/363, TC-158/401/402 and their implementation correspondences (K1–K3). The original three SR-174 acceptance clauses and parent approvals are retained. The expanded non-reuse promise has the uncovered case in finding 1; the rationale has finding 2. Authority/checkpoint completion is UNCOVERED here: convergence excludes the earlier verdict/act records, and the new children are still Drafted. This review does not attest or replace those acts.\n- Reserve argument allow/refuse table: covered by test_id_carrier.py's CLI refusal cases, including byte-identical durable-state assertions and argparse exit 2. CAS, first-use seed and lost race: covered by test_id_carrier_git.py and the independent simultaneous-process probe.\n- Sequential/stale-base disjointness and post-correction floor: covered by the corresponding real-worktree tests; the sequential regression fails on the base. Clean/conflicted fold, maximum marks, record union and receiving-tree basis: covered by the pure fold tests and `test_fold_ids_on_a_conflicted_watermark`.\n- Hand-raise authority, above-record refusal, append-only records across parents, bump/correction preservation and reservation-versus-correction grammar: covered by the new test_id_watermark.py CLI/real-history cases. Existing behavioral tests are retained.\n- Both landing callers and each landing-table row: covered by test_id_landing.py's parametrized route tests and positive controls. Reuse of an ID first minted within the lane is covered, but retirement/re-authoring of a base-live ID is UNCOVERED and fails open (finding 1). Export-ignore cannot hide a committed registry; the exact sweep-equivalence test passes in the writable scratch repository.\n- Landing fold, moved carrier, speculative drain readiness and watermark-only refresh conflict: covered by the corresponding slot, squash and refresh tests. A registry conflict beside the watermark is still refused and the merge undone; its regression fails on pre-fix code.\n- Act ledger, numbering and latch: inspected as unchanged in this diff. Dedicated act-ledger test suites are UNCOVERED by the launcher's prescribed selection; no green for those unrun suites is claimed.\n- Keep-or-mint paragraph, one-line spine-question citation, reserved-range author prompt and reserve/fold coordinator procedure: inspected at their shipped sites; test_prompts.py and dogfood-sync cover the three synchronized copies. Byte deltas match the stamps.\n- RESYNC: one entry inspected, including existing-lane reserve/renumber instructions and the no-claim squash refusal. Own IDs are reserved and pass the actual landing judgement. Naming and upgrading the other open lanes at the eventual landing is UNCOVERED/pending landing procedure, not inferred from implementation notes.\n- Generated pages: catalogue changes are generated as declared; actual landing-time regeneration of the ignored views is UNCOVERED here. The supplied batch and both prescribed strict instruments were run once; no full suite or smoke tier was run. `trace.py --strict` beyond the prescribed integrity mode is UNCOVERED, so the spec's entire final commit-bar claim is not endorsed.\n- Parent/child and interface decisions: SR-174's changed scope realizes unchanged SN-025 and SN-027; keep their acceptance and approvals. Retain LLR-153's independent serial WI-mint decision and LLR-154's independent post-merge intake timing decision: neither becomes a carrier-space allocator. Retain TC-147's WI-trigger/idempotence verification and TC-148's serial intake-placement verification, each scoped through its own LLR; neither is credited with the new carrier acceptance. TC-158 carries the additional watermark-record verification; TC-401 and TC-402 carry allocation and landing verification respectively. Further splitting these retained children by individual triggers or malformed inputs adds cases, not another independent ownership decision.\n- LLR-362 owns the shared allocation/record authority; LLR-363 owns landing admission. The fold is the same carrier projection used by these boundaries, not a second allocation policy needing a duplicate definition. Carry the IF-101 expansion to its two new callers; retain its existing reader/bump contract. Carry IF-224's shared trunk/WI parsers into reservation coverage, but correct its now-false import-scope note (finding 3). The added symbol links on LLR-141, LLR-181 and LLR-337 retain their existing behavioral contracts: shared claim parsing, byte-exact git extraction and branch containment support those contracts without substituting new policy. No surviving duplicate implementation was identified among the new functions; strict and lenient watermark readers have distinct failure policies over one shared grammar.\n- PROCESS section 5 routing for finding 1: the widened SR-174 non-reuse obligation remains sound. LLR-363's final-set-difference definition is insufficient to implement it; amend that child and TC-402 through their applicable independent authority, preserve parent acceptance/approval, and keep the enduring behavioral regressions. Adding a compensating path while retaining the child unchanged would leave the design defect in the requirement.\n\nFindings:\n\n- [MAJOR] project-trajectory/scripts/id_carrier.py:486 -> [K1, B1] Retirement reuse fails open when the spent ID was already live at the lane's base: retire SR-003 with retire.py, commit its record, then author a different SR-003 in the same lane. `_added_ids` subtracts the base-live ID, and `landing_lines` returns before consulting retirement records. Both supported landing routes admitted the probe: the slot landed the different obligation and the squash step exited 0. This violates newly widened SR-174's non-reuse acceptance; TC-402 only covers reuse of an ID first minted after the base -> Amend LLR-363 and TC-402 to preserve SR-174 and judge live identities against authoritative retirement records independently of the net-added reservation set, through the one shared landing trust boundary; extend both route regressions to a base-live ID. This is validation of authored content at the existing trust boundary (PROCESS section 3; antidote), not a compensating guard on trusted in-process state; re-attest the changed child contract and its verification -> @wi-978-owner convergence-class: yes\n- [MINOR] docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml:1000 -> [K1] The added sentence `The keep-or-mint review checks ...` puts this amendment's review receipt in SR-174's living rationale. PROCESS section 3 explicitly bars requirement rationale from carrying its own history; the user's required cross-reference belongs in the scoped review record, not normative row content -> Keep the standing concurrency/non-reuse reasoning in SR-174 and move the keep-or-mint receipt to the scoped amendment/review record, retaining the independently checked user decisions recorded above -> @wi-978-owner\n- [MINOR] docs/requirements/interfaces.toml:2078 -> [B1] IF-224 now includes trunk_name and parse_wi_id for reservations, but its retained Notes still claim the import occurs only during approval-brief rendering and that every other trace run loads no coordinator primitives. The new reserve path imports agent_common in check_work_item and _trunk_name, so the expanded interface's own scope description is false -> Carry the interface expansion into Notes, naming reservation and approval-brief use and limiting the no-import claim to the remaining paths -> @wi-978-owner\nVERDICT: CHANGES-REQUESTED findings=3\n\r\nReviewed: f7809e313a17bf145b639922b1d84192760f7a66\n\nFull-lane review of WI-978 (lane wi-978, range 2f4ebb2a5..f7809e313), convergence stance. I read no docs/reviews, docs/decisions, critique records or lane commit messages. I took pre-fix code only from `git log --format=%h` patches and `git archive` snapshots.\n\n**Verified.** I first named the failure classes to hunt, worst first:\n- an unreserved id that the landing admits (fail-open);\n- two reservations that overlap (silent wrong content);\n- a writer or fold that drops records or lowers a mark (data loss).\n\nWhat I checked and found right:\n\n- **Disjoint ranges.** `plan_reservation` starts each range one above the larger of the carrier's mark and trunk's committed mark. The carrier moves only by `update-ref <ref> <new> <old>`, with `\"\"` meaning \"the ref must not exist\" on first use. A lost swap raises `LOST_RACE` before the working file is touched. The sequential, stale-base, after-correction and lost-race cases all pass.\n- **Writers keep every record.** `bump_watermark`, `correct_watermark`, the fold and `bootstrap.raise_watermark` all keep records. `bootstrap.raise_watermark` keeps every line it does not raise.\n- **Trunk mints.** The trunk mints (intake, plan_artifacts) go through `bump_watermark`.\n- **Committed records.** These are read as a union over every baseline, so dropping a record is refused.\n- **Carrier-space sweep.** Every carrier space's registry lives under `LANDING_DIRS` (`docs/requirements`, `docs/test`). So the extracted-tree sweep cannot miss a carrier-space id.\n- **Landing check, both routes.** The slot reads the lane's delta over its merge base. The hand squash also reads the staged tree over HEAD. Both read retirement records at every tree they judge.\n- **This lane against the real carrier.** I ran the shipped check in-process (`trace.landing_id_refusal(root, \"wi-978\", <merge base>, [\"WI-978\"])`): it returns None and the fold lag is []. The carrier (`refs/heads/id-carrier`, 58c902c22) holds `LLR 362-363 (WI-978)` and `TC 401-402 (WI-978)`. The same call keyed on WI-963 refuses all four ids.\n- **Earlier fixes, confirmed against pre-fix code.** I ran the final tests against `git archive` snapshots of the code before each fix, and every one fails there:\n  - At 76b8189ff, 3 of 3 fail: the slot and hand-squash retired-id-inside-reservation cases (\"the slot admitted wi-401\"; step exit 0), and the hand squash of another lane's number after that lane landed (step exit 0).\n  - At cecb461f2, 6 of 6 fail:\n    - export-ignore hiding a registry, on both the judgement and the slot;\n    - a registry conflict beside the watermark (SystemExit on unparsable TOML);\n    - a reservation in a lane behind trunk (TC/WI hand-raise integrity findings);\n    - the carrier contributing only record-justified marks (no `carried`);\n    - the slot after trunk deletes an id at the lane's base (false refusal of SR-003).\n- **Spec amendment.** The in-lane amendment of the spec's fold paragraph matches `id_carrier.carried`.\n- **Spine sweep.** SR-174, LLR-362/363, TC-158 and TC-401/402 contradict no existing row I checked: LLR-153/154, TC-147/148/158 and SN-027 (Approved). No SN/SR cell names a carrier (K2).\n- **Text items.** PROCESS.md §4 carries keep-or-mint beside CLARITY and MEANING (line 681). spine-questions §0 cites it in one line. The terra prompt drafts only from the reserved ranges. Recipes §4 has \"reserve before drafting\" and the rebase row's fold. SKILL §4 drops the watermark clause, and both skill copies match.\n- **Untouched code.** The act ledger and `_record_act` are untouched.\n\n**Done-when coverage:**\n\n| Done-when item | Covered by |\n|---|---|\n| Spine | the registry diff |\n| Reserve table | test_id_carrier counts, work-item and refusal-writes-nothing cases |\n| CAS and first use | test_id_carrier_git first-use and lost-race cases |\n| Disjoint ranges and the correction floor | test_id_carrier_git sequential, stale-base and after-correction cases |\n| Fold, clean and conflicted | test_id_carrier fold cases; test_fold_ids_on_a_conflicted_watermark |\n| Hand-raise, record history, writers | test_id_watermark (TC-158 evidence list) |\n| Landing table, both callers | test_id_landing REFUSED parametrized on the slot and the hand squash, plus the claim, own-record, code-only and stale-base cases |\n| Landing and refresh fold | test_id_landing fold and refresh-conflict cases |\n| Acts unchanged | no act file in the diff |\n| Text | the diffs above |\n| RESYNC | RESYNC_PACK entry |\n\nTwo items are landing-time, not lane, items: regenerating the [generated] cli and interface references, and naming the other open lanes. I could not see the spine sitting's verdict within the convergence reading scope. The integrity floor (approval-immutable, registry-integrity) is clean on the tip.\n\n**Coverage-extension notes (no severity):**\n- The counts parser also admits surrounding whitespace and leading zeros (` SR=05`).\n- A hand `git merge --no-ff` of a lane on trunk is judged by neither caller. That is the spec's declared residual: only the slot and a squash are callers.\n- 19 posix-shell-gated tests were skipped on this box. The hook wiring is pinned by `test_the_pre_commit_hook_runs_the_reserved_ids_step`, and the squash step is driven through `check.py --run-steps reserved-ids`.\n\n**Commands**\n- `git rev-parse HEAD` (in C:/Projects/ai-template.wt/wi-978) -> `f7809e313a17bf145b639922b1d84192760f7a66`\n- the brief's scoped pytest command (17 files, `-n 2`, the literal `--basetemp`) -> `758 passed, 20 skipped in 1829.28s (0:30:29)`, exit 0. It ran past the shell tool's 1800 s cap, so the harness moved it to the background before it finished. I read its own output file after it completed.\n- `python project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py --strict` -> `check_trajectory: clean (986 work item(s), 872 done (88%), 34 cancelled, graph acyclic).`, exit 0\n- `python project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py --strict-integrity` -> `Traceability: SN=31 SR=138 LLR=329 TC=356 orphans=0 integrity=0 verified-mechanized=131 verified-demonstrated=7 verified-attested=0 drafts=4 budgets=4 budget-findings=0 components=4 component-findings=0 interfaces=255 interface-findings=0 ac-advisories=1 paraphrase-advisories=2. Report -> docs\\test\\report.md`, exit 0. `git status --short` in the worktree printed nothing afterwards.\n- pytest of the final retired-id and after-landing tests on a `git archive 76b8189ff` snapshot -> `3 failed, 30 deselected`\n- pytest of six final regression tests on a `git archive cecb461f2` snapshot -> `6 failed in 36.07s`\n- in-process probe of `trace.landing_id_refusal` / `landing_fold_lag` on this lane -> `refusal: None`, `lag: []`; the WI-963 key refuses LLR-362, LLR-363, TC-401 and TC-402\n- in-process probe of `id_carrier.landing_lines(set(), [], [\"WI-401\"], [(\"SR\",500,510,\"WI-401\")], [], True, \"x\")` -> `[]`; the same forged record with SR-006 added -> refused, naming the line\n- a scratch AST script cross-checking each changed function's `Implements:` LLR against that row's `module` and `code_symbol` -> the five misses in finding 2\n\n**Findings**\n\n- [MINOR] project-trajectory/scripts/id_carrier.py:486 -> landing-table row 6 (\"the landing's watermark carries a record that neither the carrier nor the parent holds -> refused\") is judged only when the landing adds a carrier-space id. `landing_lines` returns [] before the record check whenever `added` is empty. A code-only landing whose watermark carries a hand- or agent-written `# reservation: SR 500-510 (WI-NNN)` therefore lands; I confirmed this by running `landing_lines`. On trunk the line is then a committed record, which the hand-raise rule (`_mark_history_findings`) accepts as justifying a mark raised to 510, and the append-only rule keeps forever. That wastes numbers but cannot collide, since landing authority reads the carrier only. The spec's own table makes rows 1 and 6 overlap for this state, and LLR-363's first two sentences 
